/P 135 0 R
/T (Implementation and Expected Impact)
/E (Implementation and Expected Impact)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Matching Point can implement the proposed inventory system using a simple Excel-based tracker. The owner should update sales and stock information regularly, conduct weekly reviews of high-demand and Near Stockout products, and perform a monthly review of Overstock and slow-moving products. Using the 7-day moving average along with current stock status will support more informed purchasing decisions. The approach is expected to reduce unnecessary inventory accumulation, lower the risk of stockouts, release working capital locked in slow-moving products, and improve the balance between product availability and inventory cost.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 135 0 R
/T (4.3 Overall Recommendation)
/E (4.3 Overall Recommendation)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Based on the combined findings, Matching Point should move from intuition-based inventory decisions towards a simple data-driven inventory management system. The business should give priority to high-contribution products, monitor turnover and locked capital, use recent sales demand for replenishment, and regularly identify Near Stockout and Overstock products.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (The proposed approach does not require expensive software and can be implemented through a structured Excel-based inventory system. Weekly monitoring of high-demand products, monthly review of slow-moving and overstocked products, and the use of recent sales trends for replenishment can help Matching Point gradually reduce locked working capital and unnecessary inventory costs while maintaining better availability of frequently demanded products.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
